1
00:00:00,333 --> 00:00:01,912
Two crew members dead.

2
00:00:01,912 --> 00:00:03,275
A bomber in flames.

3
00:00:03,275 --> 00:00:06,074
Metal debris that no one could explain.

4
00:00:06,074 --> 00:00:11,888
And a man in a black suit who knocked on a door and told a witness to stay quiet.

5
00:00:11,888 --> 00:00:15,333
This is the case file nobody wanted you to open.

6
00:00:15,800 --> 00:00:17,580
June nineteen forty-seven.

7
00:00:17,580 --> 00:00:21,414
Six doughnut-shaped craft above Puget Sound, Washington.

8
00:00:21,414 --> 00:00:22,989
One of them in trouble.

9
00:00:22,989 --> 00:00:24,016
Falling debris.

10
00:00:24,016 --> 00:00:29,700
And three days later, the whole world would get a name for what people were seeing.

11
00:00:30,166 --> 00:00:32,613
Tonight we reopen it as a cold case.

12
00:00:32,613 --> 00:00:38,933
Every witness, every fragment, every claim, put on the table and tested against the evidence.

13
00:00:39,399 --> 00:00:41,255
We will meet the witnesses.

14
00:00:41,255 --> 00:00:44,278
We will follow the bomber to its crash site.

15
00:00:44,278 --> 00:00:52,866
And we will ask the question investigators asked at the time: was this the first great UFO cover-up, or the first great hoax?

16
00:00:53,333 --> 00:00:55,155
Every claim gets a score.

17
00:00:55,155 --> 00:00:56,759
At the end, a verdict.

18
00:00:56,759 --> 00:00:59,091
Case solved, or case still open.

19
00:00:59,091 --> 00:00:59,966
Let's begin.

20
00:01:04,433 --> 00:01:05,072
File one.

21
00:01:05,072 --> 00:01:06,065
The complaint.

22
00:01:06,065 --> 00:01:12,027
Tacoma, Washington, nineteen forty-seven, a busy port of timber, steel and smelters.

23
00:01:12,027 --> 00:01:19,266
The Pacific Northwest had just come out of the war, and it was full of men who knew boats and engines.

24
00:01:19,733 --> 00:01:24,825
Harold Dahl worked harbor patrol, and he salvaged floating logs for a living.

25
00:01:24,825 --> 00:01:32,233
On the twenty-first of June he took his boat out near Maury Island with his young son, his dog, and two crewmen.

26
00:01:32,700 --> 00:01:41,004
Around two in the afternoon, Dahl said, he saw six large doughnut-shaped objects about two thousand feet above the boat.

27
00:01:41,004 --> 00:01:42,457
Five circled a sixth.

28
00:01:42,457 --> 00:01:46,333
Each looked like a ring, with a hole through the middle.

29
00:01:46,799 --> 00:01:50,266
Dahl said they were around one hundred feet across.

30
00:01:50,266 --> 00:01:51,897
Portholes along the rim.

31
00:01:51,897 --> 00:01:52,509
Metallic.

32
00:01:52,509 --> 00:01:55,432
And the middle one seemed to be in trouble.

33
00:01:55,432 --> 00:01:56,111
It slowed.

34
00:01:56,111 --> 00:01:56,859
It hovered.

35
00:01:56,859 --> 00:01:58,966
And the others came down to it.

36
00:01:59,433 --> 00:02:03,829
Then, Dahl said, the damaged craft began to throw out debris.

37
00:02:03,829 --> 00:02:10,747
Thin white metal, like aluminium sheet, and heavy grey lumps that looked like lava rock or slag.

38
00:02:10,747 --> 00:02:12,333
It rained on the boat.

39
00:02:12,800 --> 00:02:15,242
Dahl said his son's arm was hurt.

40
00:02:15,242 --> 00:02:18,277
His dog was killed by the falling pieces.

41
00:02:18,277 --> 00:02:19,684
The radio was dead.

42
00:02:19,684 --> 00:02:23,533
Then the objects rose, and left toward the open sea.

43
00:02:24,000 --> 00:02:25,652
Dahl came back to shore.

44
00:02:25,652 --> 00:02:27,718
He took his son to the doctor.

45
00:02:27,718 --> 00:02:29,233
And he buried the dog.

46
00:02:29,233 --> 00:02:34,466
The next day, he told his boss, a man named Fred Crisman, what had happened.

47
00:02:34,933 --> 00:02:35,531
File two.

48
00:02:35,531 --> 00:02:36,462
The witnesses.

49
00:02:36,462 --> 00:02:40,783
Fred Crisman was Dahl's superior, and he said he had seen it too.

50
00:02:40,783 --> 00:02:46,900
He went out in a boat the next day and said he found more of the same wreckage on the beach.

51
00:02:47,366 --> 00:02:49,890
Here is what makes this case unusual.

52
00:02:49,890 --> 00:02:51,459
Dahl was not a drifter.

53
00:02:51,459 --> 00:02:53,778
He had a job, a boat and a family.

54
00:02:53,778 --> 00:02:57,666
And at first, he did not want to talk to anyone about it.

55
00:02:58,133 --> 00:03:03,196
Then, on the twenty-fourth of June, something happened that changed everything.

56
00:03:03,196 --> 00:03:10,566
A pilot named Kenneth Arnold was flying near Mount Rainier, and he saw nine bright shapes skimming through the sky.

57
00:03:11,033 --> 00:03:15,043
Arnold said they moved like saucers skipping on water.

58
00:03:15,043 --> 00:03:20,465
A reporter turned that into two words, and they spread around the planet.

59
00:03:20,465 --> 00:03:21,579
Flying saucers.

60
00:03:21,579 --> 00:03:23,733
The modern UFO era had begun.

61
00:03:24,199 --> 00:03:28,462
Within days, newspapers across America were full of sightings.

62
00:03:28,462 --> 00:03:35,199
And in Tacoma, Dahl's story, which had been a private secret, suddenly looked like part of a wave.

63
00:03:35,666 --> 00:03:38,129
One man in particular was listening.

64
00:03:38,129 --> 00:03:47,500
Ray Palmer edited Amazing Stories, a pulp magazine of science fiction, and he had a habit of printing the strangest claims he could find.

65
00:03:47,966 --> 00:03:55,976
On the twenty-eighth of July, Palmer wired two hundred dollars to Kenneth Arnold, to go to Tacoma and investigate.

66
00:03:55,976 --> 00:03:57,451
Remember that detail.

67
00:03:57,451 --> 00:03:59,699
Money was now part of the story.

68
00:04:00,166 --> 00:04:00,950
File three.

69
00:04:00,950 --> 00:04:01,734
The debris.

70
00:04:01,734 --> 00:04:09,218
Arnold flew to Tacoma, and he brought a second witness, a United Airlines pilot named Captain Emil Smith.

71
00:04:09,218 --> 00:04:13,566
Both men were skeptical, but both wanted to see the evidence.

72
00:04:14,033 --> 00:04:16,375
Dahl showed them a cardboard box.

73
00:04:16,375 --> 00:04:21,415
Inside were chunks of grey porous stone, and some thin metallic scraps.

74
00:04:21,415 --> 00:04:26,100
He said these came from the doughnut that fell apart over the bay.

75
00:04:26,566 --> 00:04:29,210
Anyone who has seen slag will know it.

76
00:04:29,210 --> 00:04:33,524
Volcanic looking, bubbled, heavy, a waste product of smelting.

77
00:04:33,524 --> 00:04:38,533
And Tacoma was home to one of the biggest smelters on the Pacific coast.

78
00:04:39,000 --> 00:04:41,743
That fact is a problem for the story.

79
00:04:41,743 --> 00:04:44,635
Slag was everywhere around Puget Sound.

80
00:04:44,635 --> 00:04:46,711
It was used in construction.

81
00:04:46,711 --> 00:04:49,899
And a gravel pit sat close to Maury Island.

82
00:04:50,366 --> 00:04:52,612
The skeptic's reading is simple.

83
00:04:52,612 --> 00:05:01,666
Take ordinary waste rock from a local pit, show it to a pilot who is hungry for a sensation, and let the imagination do the rest.

84
00:05:02,133 --> 00:05:03,828
But Arnold was not sure.

85
00:05:03,828 --> 00:05:07,784
He and Smith spent two tense days with Dahl and Crisman.

86
00:05:07,784 --> 00:05:14,566
By the end, something had unsettled them, and he sent a message to two Army officers he trusted.

87
00:05:15,033 --> 00:05:23,787
Captain William Davidson and Lieutenant Frank Brown, from Army Air Forces intelligence at Hamilton Field in California, flew north.

88
00:05:23,787 --> 00:05:28,733
They interviewed the men, and they took a box of fragments back with them.

89
00:05:29,199 --> 00:05:32,404
They loaded the samples into a B-25 bomber.

90
00:05:32,404 --> 00:05:35,907
A twin-engine plane that had served in the war.

91
00:05:35,907 --> 00:05:39,633
It was the last time those two men would ever fly.

92
00:05:40,100 --> 00:05:40,871
File four.

93
00:05:40,871 --> 00:05:41,643
The crash.

94
00:05:41,643 --> 00:05:49,821
Early in the morning of the first of August, nineteen forty-seven, the B-25 was flying home to California.

95
00:05:49,821 --> 00:05:53,833
Near Kelso, Washington, the left engine caught fire.

96
00:05:54,300 --> 00:05:55,378
The fire spread.

97
00:05:55,378 --> 00:05:56,591
A wing came apart.

98
00:05:56,591 --> 00:06:00,770
The crew chief and a passenger jumped, and they landed safely.

99
00:06:00,770 --> 00:06:06,566
But Davidson and Brown, the officers carrying the fragments, were killed in the wreck.

100
00:06:07,033 --> 00:06:08,395
The Army moved fast.

101
00:06:08,395 --> 00:06:10,439
Investigators sealed the site.

102
00:06:10,439 --> 00:06:15,820
And the box of samples that had been aboard was never officially accounted for.

103
00:06:15,820 --> 00:06:19,566
That gap in the paperwork is where the legend was born.

104
00:06:20,033 --> 00:06:21,867
Then came the phone calls.

105
00:06:21,867 --> 00:06:29,907
Reporters in Tacoma said an anonymous man rang them hours before the news was public, and named the dead officers.

106
00:06:29,907 --> 00:06:33,433
Some said he claimed the plane had been shot down.

107
00:06:33,899 --> 00:06:36,130
If that is true, it is chilling.

108
00:06:36,130 --> 00:06:37,664
But here is the catch.

109
00:06:37,664 --> 00:06:40,383
That claim comes from later retellings.

110
00:06:40,383 --> 00:06:46,866
In nineteen forty-seven, nobody wrote down who the caller was, or how he got his information.

111
00:06:47,333 --> 00:06:50,222
The official finding was dull and sad.

112
00:06:50,222 --> 00:06:57,598
An exhaust stack on the left engine had overheated and set fire to the wing, and the wing failed.

113
00:06:57,598 --> 00:07:01,933
B-25s of that type had a known problem with exactly that.

114
00:07:02,399 --> 00:07:06,736
Two later investigators have argued the plane was fine.

115
00:07:06,736 --> 00:07:12,491
Others have noted that the same flaw had grounded or damaged other B-25s.

116
00:07:12,491 --> 00:07:16,433
The cause was ordinary, and sadly well documented.

117
00:07:16,899 --> 00:07:23,291
Whatever happened that night, two officers died doing their job, and that should not be forgotten.

118
00:07:23,291 --> 00:07:27,399
It is also the only part of this story that everyone agrees on.

119
00:07:27,866 --> 00:07:28,551
File five.

120
00:07:28,551 --> 00:07:29,715
The man in black.

121
00:07:29,715 --> 00:07:41,766
Dahl said that the morning after the incident, a stranger in a black suit took him to breakfast in a diner in Tacoma, and calmly told him what had happened, down to the detail.

122
00:07:42,233 --> 00:07:45,263
The man, Dahl said, warned him not to talk.

123
00:07:45,263 --> 00:07:49,842
Told him bad things would happen to him and his family if he did.

124
00:07:49,842 --> 00:07:50,758
Then he left.

125
00:07:50,758 --> 00:07:53,999
It is the earliest man in black story we have.

126
00:07:54,466 --> 00:08:01,729
Pop culture later turned the men in black into sleek agents with sunglasses and memory-wiping devices.

127
00:08:01,729 --> 00:08:06,500
But the first one is just a man, a suit, a breakfast, and a threat.

128
00:08:06,966 --> 00:08:09,799
And this is the problem for the evidence.

129
00:08:09,799 --> 00:08:11,526
We have only Dahl's word.

130
00:08:11,526 --> 00:08:15,948
There is no diner receipt, no witness, no description of a face.

131
00:08:15,948 --> 00:08:19,333
The first telling that survives came years later.

132
00:08:19,800 --> 00:08:22,081
What we do have is an FBI file.

133
00:08:22,081 --> 00:08:32,166
Agents interviewed Dahl and Crisman in August, after the crash, because the Army wanted to know what the two men had really said and why.

134
00:08:32,633 --> 00:08:37,572
According to the report, Dahl and Crisman admitted the fragments were just slag.

135
00:08:37,572 --> 00:08:44,733
They said the story had been a hoax, that they had embellished it for Palmer, and that they gave him what he wanted.

136
00:08:45,200 --> 00:08:49,660
Dahl later disputed the confession and said the FBI had it wrong.

137
00:08:49,660 --> 00:08:52,817
He kept to his story for the rest of his life.

138
00:08:52,817 --> 00:08:56,866
And that brings us to the question every cold case reaches.

139
00:08:57,333 --> 00:08:58,002
File six.

140
00:08:58,002 --> 00:08:59,342
The hoax question.

141
00:08:59,342 --> 00:09:01,053
Let's weigh it plainly.

142
00:09:01,053 --> 00:09:04,401
On one side, a story told by two salvage men.

143
00:09:04,401 --> 00:09:10,799
On the other, a magazine editor with a budget, and a market hungry for flying saucers.

144
00:09:11,266 --> 00:09:14,280
Ray Palmer sold stories of strange wonders.

145
00:09:14,280 --> 00:09:24,233
The saucer craze was the best news his readers had ever had, and a witness who had been hit by falling saucer wreckage was a perfect headline.

146
00:09:24,700 --> 00:09:26,669
Now the physical evidence.

147
00:09:26,669 --> 00:09:31,215
A military counter-intelligence agent inspected Dahl's boat.

148
00:09:31,215 --> 00:09:35,078
He found old, weathered paint, and no fresh damage.

149
00:09:35,078 --> 00:09:37,200
No dents from falling rocks.

150
00:09:37,200 --> 00:09:38,866
No sign of a disaster.

151
00:09:39,333 --> 00:09:40,994
Then there is the dog.

152
00:09:40,994 --> 00:09:44,241
The story says it was killed by the debris.

153
00:09:44,241 --> 00:09:51,114
But nobody found a grave that anyone could verify, and nobody saw the animal alive or dead.

154
00:09:51,114 --> 00:09:52,700
A story with no body.

155
00:09:53,166 --> 00:09:54,561
Next, the sightings.

156
00:09:54,561 --> 00:09:58,396
Were there other reports of ring-shaped craft that day?

157
00:09:58,396 --> 00:09:59,790
None in the records.

158
00:09:59,790 --> 00:10:06,833
In an age when thousands of people phoned police about saucers, nobody else on that bay saw anything.

159
00:10:07,299 --> 00:10:10,638
The Army and the FBI looked, and wrote it off.

160
00:10:10,638 --> 00:10:15,284
The Air Force's own Project Blue Book later listed it as a hoax.

161
00:10:15,284 --> 00:10:19,566
It was among the first cases in the program to be so named.

162
00:10:20,033 --> 00:10:22,686
But hoax does not mean nothing happened.

163
00:10:22,686 --> 00:10:25,338
Something did frighten Arnold and Smith.

164
00:10:25,338 --> 00:10:26,864
And the bomber crashed.

165
00:10:26,864 --> 00:10:32,699
The mistake many retellings make is to join those two facts, as if one caused the other.

166
00:10:33,166 --> 00:10:34,128
A real crash.

167
00:10:34,128 --> 00:10:35,312
A fake sighting.

168
00:10:35,312 --> 00:10:39,899
The web that links them is drawn by people who wanted a story.

169
00:10:39,899 --> 00:10:42,266
The evidence draws no such line.

170
00:10:42,733 --> 00:10:43,507
File seven.

171
00:10:43,507 --> 00:10:44,281
The legacy.

172
00:10:44,281 --> 00:10:59,133
Years later, Fred Crisman reappeared in the news, this time in nineteen sixty-eight, when a New Orleans prosecutor named Jim Garrison summoned a Tacoma man of that name as a witness in the Kennedy investigation.

173
00:10:59,600 --> 00:11:01,339
Was it the same Crisman?

174
00:11:01,339 --> 00:11:03,224
Some say yes, some say no.

175
00:11:03,224 --> 00:11:12,213
Either way, a legend grew: the man from the Maury Island incident, now tied to the most famous assassination of the century.

176
00:11:12,213 --> 00:11:14,533
That is a theory, with no proof.

177
00:11:15,000 --> 00:11:17,619
And the men in black never went away.

178
00:11:17,619 --> 00:11:21,231
They became movie stars, then a joke, then a brand.

179
00:11:21,231 --> 00:11:26,966
All of it trails back, in part, to one breakfast in Tacoma that nobody witnessed.

180
00:11:27,433 --> 00:11:30,045
Modern researchers have kept digging.

181
00:11:30,045 --> 00:11:33,929
Some argue the Army did hide something about the crash.

182
00:11:33,929 --> 00:11:41,766
Others, like the investigators who combed the FBI papers, found a story that collapses under its own documents.

183
00:11:42,233 --> 00:11:51,949
If aliens did visit Puget Sound in nineteen forty-seven, they left behind a lot of slag, a dead plane, and not one clear photograph.

184
00:11:51,949 --> 00:11:52,611
No craft.

185
00:11:52,611 --> 00:11:56,733
No fragment that any lab ever tied to something unknown.

186
00:11:57,200 --> 00:11:59,718
Here is the test that would settle it.

187
00:11:59,718 --> 00:12:02,105
A single piece of the box, in a lab.

188
00:12:02,105 --> 00:12:08,866
The bomber crash made that impossible, and ever since, the missing samples have kept the legend alive.

189
00:12:09,333 --> 00:12:10,574
But think about it.

190
00:12:10,574 --> 00:12:16,126
If the samples are gone, the case can never be proven, and it can never be disproven.

191
00:12:16,126 --> 00:12:19,000
That is exactly the shape of a perfect myth.

192
00:12:19,466 --> 00:12:20,320
Case review.

193
00:12:20,320 --> 00:12:23,166
Time to score the Maury Island incident.

194
00:12:23,633 --> 00:12:29,299
A B-twenty-five that crashed near Kelso, killing two officers: evidence.

195
00:12:29,299 --> 00:12:33,785
Dahl and Crisman telling a story to Ray Palmer: evidence.

196
00:12:33,785 --> 00:12:39,766
A Dahl and Crisman statement to the FBI calling the debris slag: documented.

197
00:12:40,233 --> 00:12:43,405
Six craft over the bay: no second witness.

198
00:12:43,405 --> 00:12:46,426
A man in black at breakfast: unverified.

199
00:12:46,426 --> 00:12:49,372
A cover-up of alien metal: no evidence.

200
00:12:49,372 --> 00:12:54,433
The truth lies in the slag, the money and the single, tragic crash.

201
00:12:54,899 --> 00:12:55,442
Verdict.

202
00:12:55,442 --> 00:12:57,610
Case closed, with one open door.

203
00:12:57,610 --> 00:13:07,166
The sighting was almost certainly a hoax, but the crash was real, and the story it started still shapes how we picture a government cover-up.

204
00:13:07,633 --> 00:13:11,409
In the end, Maury Island is not a story about aliens.

205
00:13:11,409 --> 00:13:17,466
It is a story about how fast a rumour can grow when the world is ready to believe it.

206
00:13:17,933 --> 00:13:23,776
Two men died in nineteen forty-seven, and their names deserve better than a legend.

207
00:13:23,776 --> 00:13:25,114
Davidson and Brown.

208
00:13:25,114 --> 00:13:26,100
Remember them.

209
00:13:26,566 --> 00:13:29,354
Next case: a book nobody has ever read.

210
00:13:29,354 --> 00:13:36,289
Hundreds of pages, written in a script no one can decode, and every cracker who tried has failed.

211
00:13:36,289 --> 00:13:37,933
The Voynich manuscript.

212
00:13:38,399 --> 00:13:42,961
Was it a hoax, a cipher, a lost language, or something stranger?

213
00:13:42,961 --> 00:13:45,100
The next file is already open.

214
00:13:45,566 --> 00:13:48,345
What do you think happened over Maury Island?

215
00:13:48,345 --> 00:13:53,533
Tell us in the comments, and subscribe for the next ancient mystery put to the test.
